</style>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/4.7.0/css/font-awesome.min.css">
</head>
<body class="article toc2 toc-right">
<div id="header">
<h1>Sets and form fragments</h1>
<div id="toc" class="toc2">
<div id="toctitle">Table of Contents</div>
<ul class="sectlevel1">
<li><a href="#repeat_a_group_of_fields">Repeat a group of fields</a>
<ul class="sectlevel2">
<li><a href="#task_add_filmography_to_artist">Task: Add filmography to Artist</a></li>
<li><a href="#task_query_the_filmography">Task: Query the filmography</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="#choose_an_option_with_its_own_fields">Choose an option with its own fields</a>
<ul class="sectlevel2">
<li><a href="#task_add_feeding_behavior_to_animal">Task: Add feeding behavior to Animal</a></li>
<li><a href="#task_query_the_selected_option">Task: Query the selected option</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="#reuse_fields_across_options">Reuse fields across options</a>
<ul class="sectlevel2">
<li><a href="#task_create_the_distribution_form_fragment">Task: Create the distribution form fragment</a></li>
<li><a href="#task_add_distribution_to_animal">Task: Add distribution to Animal</a></li>
<li><a href="#task_query_the_distribution">Task: Query the distribution</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="#visual_grouping_and_further_reading">Visual grouping and further reading</a></li>
<li><a href="#coming_up">Coming up</a></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="content">
<div id="preamble">
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>In this chapter, you&#8217;ll add filmography entries to your artists, describe animals with selectable options, and reuse fields with a form fragment.
After each addition, you&#8217;ll enter content and query it through Guillotine.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="repeat_a_group_of_fields"><a class="link" href="#repeat_a_group_of_fields">Repeat a group of fields</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>In the previous chapter, <code>otherNames</code> repeated a single TextLine.
An <em>item set</em> groups several fields into one object and can repeat that whole group.
For an artist&#8217;s filmography, each entry will contain a title and a year.
These entries belong to the artist; they are not separate content items like the referenced favorite animal.</p>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="task_add_filmography_to_artist"><a class="link" href="#task_add_filmography_to_artist">Task: Add filmography to Artist</a></h3>
<div class="olist arabic">
<ol class="arabic">
<li>
<p><strong>Define filmography entries</strong> - Append this item set to the Artist form, after <code>favoriteAnimal</code>:</p>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="title">src/main/resources/cms/content-types/artist/artist.yaml</div>
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-yaml" data-lang="yaml">  - type: "ItemSet"
    name: "filmography"
    label: "Filmography"
    occurrences:
      min: 0
      max: 0
    items:
      - type: "TextLine"
        name: "title"
        label: "Title"
        occurrences:
          min: 1
          max: 1
      - type: "Long"
        name: "year"
        label: "Year"
        occurrences:
          min: 1
          max: 1
        min: 0</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Add Cardi B&#8217;s films</strong> - Save the schema and let your running <code>enonic dev</code> watcher update the app.
Reopen Cardi B in Content Studio and add two Filmography entries:</p>
<table class="tableblock frame-all grid-all stretch">
<colgroup>
<col style="width: 50%;">
<col style="width: 50%;">
</colgroup>
<thead>
<tr>
<th class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">Title</th>
<th class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">Year</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">Hustlers</p></td>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">2019</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">F9</p></td>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">2021</p></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Save the filmography</strong> - Use <strong>Expand all</strong> if you want to view both entries together, then save the artist.
The entries are part of the same draft as Cardi B&#8217;s other fields.</p>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="images/sets-filmography.png" alt="Filmography item set with Hustlers from 2019 and F9 from 2021" width="527">
</div>
<div class="title">Figure 1. Two filmography entries on Cardi B</div>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The item set defines the structure and limits of each entry:</p>
</div>
<div class="ulist">
<ul>
<li>
<p><code>items</code> contains the fields in each filmography entry.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The item set&#8217;s <code>occurrences</code> allows any number of entries, including none.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Each entry requires both a <code>title</code> and a <code>year</code>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><code>Long</code> stores the year as an integer.
The <code>min: 0</code> directly on <code>year</code> limits its numeric value; <code>occurrences.min</code> controls whether a value is required.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="task_query_the_filmography"><a class="link" href="#task_query_the_filmography">Task: Query the filmography</a></h3>
<div class="olist arabic">
<ol class="arabic">
<li>
<p><strong>Fetch the filmography</strong> - Open Query Playground for "My First Project" and keep <strong>Draft</strong> selected throughout this chapter.
Fetch Cardi B by path and select the fields within <code>filmography</code>:</p>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-graphql" data-lang="graphql">{
  guillotine {
    get(key: "/artists/cardi-b") {
      displayName
      ... on com_example_dev101_Artist {
        data {
          filmography {
            title
            year
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The <code>data.guillotine.get</code> object contains:</p>
</div>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-json" data-lang="json">{
  "displayName": "Cardi B",
  "data": {
    "filmography": [
      { "title": "Hustlers", "year": 2019 },
      { "title": "F9", "year": 2021 }
    ]
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Guillotine generates a GraphQL object type for the item set&#8217;s fields.
Because <code>filmography</code> allows multiple occurrences, it returns a list of these objects, even when you enter just one film.
You can select <code>title</code> and <code>year</code> directly inside each entry, without another inline fragment.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="choose_an_option_with_its_own_fields"><a class="link" href="#choose_an_option_with_its_own_fields">Choose an option with its own fields</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>An <em>option set</em> lets editors choose from named options, each of which can contain its own form items.
For a deliberately simplified animal diet, you&#8217;ll offer Herbivore and Carnivore.
Only Carnivore needs a list of prey.</p>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="task_add_feeding_behavior_to_animal"><a class="link" href="#task_add_feeding_behavior_to_animal">Task: Add feeding behavior to Animal</a></h3>
<div class="olist arabic">
<ol class="arabic">
<li>
<p><strong>Define the feeding options</strong> - Append this option set to the Animal form, after <code>images</code>:</p>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="title">src/main/resources/cms/content-types/animal/animal.yaml</div>
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-yaml" data-lang="yaml">  - type: "OptionSet"
    name: "feedingBehavior"
    label: "Feeding behavior"
    selection:
      min: 1
      max: 1
    options:
      - name: "herbivore"
        label: "Herbivore"
        helpText: "Subsists primarily on plant material."
      - name: "carnivore"
        label: "Carnivore"
        helpText: "Subsists primarily on animal tissue and meat."
        items:
          - type: "TextLine"
            name: "prey"
            label: "Prey"
            occurrences:
              min: 0
              max: 0</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Update the animal forms</strong> - Save the schema and let the watcher update the app, then reopen your animals in Content Studio.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Describe a carnivore</strong> - For Lion, add Feeding behavior, select <strong>Carnivore</strong>, and enter "Zebra" and "Giraffe" as separate Prey values.
Save the animal.</p>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="images/sets-feeding-behavior.png" alt="Feeding behavior option set with Carnivore selected and Zebra and Giraffe entered as prey" width="527">
</div>
<div class="title">Figure 2. Lion&#8217;s feeding behavior with two prey values</div>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Describe a herbivore</strong> - For Reindeer, select <strong>Herbivore</strong> and save.
Notice that there is no Prey field for this option.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>There are two different limits to keep in mind:</p>
</div>
<div class="ulist">
<ul>
<li>
<p><code>occurrences</code> controls how many instances of the whole option set can exist.
Here it is omitted, so feeding behavior is optional and can occur at most once.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><code>selection</code> controls how many options can be selected within an instance.
Here both limits are <code>1</code>, so an editor adding feeding behavior must choose exactly one option.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The <code>herbivore</code> option has no extra fields.
The <code>carnivore</code> option contains a repeatable <code>prey</code> field.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="task_query_the_selected_option"><a class="link" href="#task_query_the_selected_option">Task: Query the selected option</a></h3>
<div class="olist arabic">
<ol class="arabic">
<li>
<p><strong>Fetch Lion&#8217;s feeding behavior</strong> - Run this query for Lion:</p>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-graphql" data-lang="graphql">{
  guillotine {
    get(key: "/animals/lion") {
      displayName
      ... on com_example_dev101_Animal {
        data {
          feedingBehavior {
            _selected
            carnivore {
              prey
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The <code>data.guillotine.get</code> object contains:</p>
</div>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-json" data-lang="json">{
  "displayName": "Lion",
  "data": {
    "feedingBehavior": {
      "_selected": "carnivore",
      "carnivore": {
        "prey": ["Zebra", "Giraffe"]
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Compare the herbivore option</strong> - Change the key to <code>/animals/reindeer</code> and run the query again.
The selected option is now <code>"herbivore"</code>, and <code>carnivore</code> is <code>null</code>.
Use <code>_selected</code> to identify the choice even when it has no extra fields, as with Herbivore.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The <code>_selected</code> field tells you which option was chosen, using its schema name rather than its label.
For this single selection, it is a string.
The <code>carnivore</code> object contains that option&#8217;s fields.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="reuse_fields_across_options"><a class="link" href="#reuse_fields_across_options">Reuse fields across options</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Next, you&#8217;ll record where an animal lives.
An animal may live on several continents, so this option set will allow multiple selections.
Each selected continent needs its own Notes field.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>A <em>form fragment</em> defines form items once so you can include them in several places.
You&#8217;ll use one here to give all seven continent options the same field definition.</p>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="task_create_the_distribution_form_fragment"><a class="link" href="#task_create_the_distribution_form_fragment">Task: Create the distribution form fragment</a></h3>
<div class="olist arabic">
<ol class="arabic">
<li>
<p><strong>Define the shared Notes field</strong> - Create this file in your app:</p>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="title">src/main/resources/cms/form-fragments/distributionData/distributionData.yaml</div>
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-yaml" data-lang="yaml">kind: "FormFragment"
title: "Distribution data"
form:
  - type: "TextArea"
    name: "notes"
    label: "Notes"</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>As with content types, the folder and filename match the schema name: <code>distributionData</code>.
Its <code>form</code> contains the fields to insert wherever the fragment is included.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="task_add_distribution_to_animal"><a class="link" href="#task_add_distribution_to_animal">Task: Add distribution to Animal</a></h3>
<div class="olist arabic">
<ol class="arabic">
<li>
<p><strong>Include the fragment for each continent</strong> - Append this option set to the Animal form, after <code>feedingBehavior</code>:</p>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="title">src/main/resources/cms/content-types/animal/animal.yaml</div>
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-yaml" data-lang="yaml">  - type: "OptionSet"
    name: "distribution"
    label: "Distribution across continents"
    helpText: "Continents where the animal can be found in the wild"
    selection:
      min: 0
      max: 7
    options:
      - name: "africa"
        label: "Africa"
        items:
          - include: "distributionData"
      - name: "antarctica"
        label: "Antarctica"
        items:
          - include: "distributionData"
      - name: "asia"
        label: "Asia"
        items:
          - include: "distributionData"
      - name: "australia"
        label: "Australia"
        items:
          - include: "distributionData"
      - name: "europe"
        label: "Europe"
        items:
          - include: "distributionData"
      - name: "northAmerica"
        label: "North America"
        items:
          - include: "distributionData"
      - name: "southAmerica"
        label: "South America"
        items:
          - include: "distributionData"</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Enter Lion&#8217;s distribution</strong> - Save both files, let the watcher update the app, and reopen Lion.
In Distribution across continents, click <b class="button">Add</b>, select Africa and Asia, and enter these notes:</p>
<table class="tableblock frame-all grid-all stretch">
<colgroup>
<col style="width: 25%;">
<col style="width: 75%;">
</colgroup>
<thead>
<tr>
<th class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">Continent</th>
<th class="tableblock halign-left valign-top">Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">Africa</p></td>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">Found in parts of sub-Saharan Africa.</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">Asia</p></td>
<td class="tableblock halign-left valign-top"><p class="tableblock">Found in Gujarat, India.</p></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Save the distribution</strong> - Save the animal.</p>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="images/sets-distribution.png" alt="Distribution option set with Africa and Asia selected, each showing its own Notes field" width="527">
</div>
<div class="title">Figure 3. Lion&#8217;s distribution with separate notes for each selected continent</div>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p><code>selection.min: 0</code> and <code>selection.max: 7</code> allow any number of the seven continents to be selected.
Each <code>include: "distributionData"</code> inserts the fragment&#8217;s Notes field into that option.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The fragment shares the field definition, not its values: Africa and Asia each have their own notes.
Changing the fragment updates the form wherever it is included, and it adds no extra level to the stored data or GraphQL response.
A CMS form fragment is also separate from a GraphQL fragment, which groups fields in a query.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="task_query_the_distribution"><a class="link" href="#task_query_the_distribution">Task: Query the distribution</a></h3>
<div class="olist arabic">
<ol class="arabic">
<li>
<p><strong>Fetch the distribution notes</strong> - Run this query to fetch both selected continents and their notes:</p>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-graphql" data-lang="graphql">{
  guillotine {
    get(key: "/animals/lion") {
      displayName
      ... on com_example_dev101_Animal {
        data {
          distribution {
            _selected
            africa {
              notes
            }
            asia {
              notes
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The <code>data.guillotine.get</code> object contains:</p>
</div>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-json" data-lang="json">{
  "displayName": "Lion",
  "data": {
    "distribution": {
      "_selected": ["africa", "asia"],
      "africa": {
        "notes": "Found in parts of sub-Saharan Africa."
      },
      "asia": {
        "notes": "Found in Gujarat, India."
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>This time, <code>_selected</code> is a list because the option set allows multiple selections.
The whole <code>distribution</code> value is still one object: the number of selected options is independent of the number of occurrences of the set.
The fragment&#8217;s <code>notes</code> field appears directly inside each continent, with no <code>distributionData</code> wrapper.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="visual_grouping_and_further_reading"><a class="link" href="#visual_grouping_and_further_reading">Visual grouping and further reading</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>If you only want to organize a form visually, a <em>field set</em> puts a heading around a group of form items without changing their data structure.
It has a label and nested <code>items</code>, but no property name of its own.
For example, grouping Artist&#8217;s existing fields in a field set would leave their GraphQL paths unchanged.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The CMS reference covers the additional settings and nesting possibilities for <a href="https://developer.enonic.com/docs/cms/stable/schemas/form-items/field-set">field sets</a>, <a href="https://developer.enonic.com/docs/cms/stable/schemas/form-items/item-set">item sets</a>, <a href="https://developer.enonic.com/docs/cms/stable/schemas/form-items/option-set">option sets</a> and <a href="https://developer.enonic.com/docs/cms/stable/schemas/form-fragments">form fragments</a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="coming_up"><a class="link" href="#coming_up">Coming up</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>You&#8217;ve repeated structured filmography entries, selected options with their own fields, reused a form fragment, and queried each addition through Guillotine.
In the next chapter, you&#8217;ll add <a href="rich-text.ahtml">rich text</a>, including links, images and macros.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id="footer">
<div id="footer-text">
Last updated 2026-10-29 08:36:45 UTC
</div>
</div>
</body>
</html>